SUMMARY:[Federal Practice Areas] US Department of Labor obtains consent or
 der against retirement plan fiduciaries of defunct Chicago law firm for fa
 iling to distribute $6.4M in assets
DESCRIPTION:CHICAGO – Following a complaint filed by the U.S. Departme
 nt of Labor\, a defunct Chicago law firm’s retirement plan distributed $
 6\,498\,858 in retirement plan assets to 40 employees. The plan’s fiduci
 aries failed to properly terminate the plan and disburse those funds as re
 quired by law.On Aug. 18\, 2026\, the U.S. District Court for the Northern
  District of Illinois\, Eastern Division\, entered a consent order resolvi
 ng the department’s claims against the McCullough\, Campbell & Lane LLP\
 , and the firm’s capital partners David L. Joslyn\, John W. McCullough\,
  F. Dennis Nelson\, and Paul S. Turner. The department alleged that the de
 fendants violated the Employee Retirement Income Security Act by failing 
 to follow a 2019 partnership resolution. The resolution required them to t
 erminate the firm’s retirement plan and distribute the plan’s assets t
 o employees. The defendants have provided proof that plan’s assets were
  distributed to participants\, except for approximately $13\,861 in the em
 ployer-level plan accounts. The consent order requires the plan’s record
 keeper to distribute those funds to its participants and to terminate the 
 plan. “After their law firm closed\, Turner\, McCullough\, Nelson\, and
  Joslyn failed to provide employees with their promised retirement benefit
 s\,” said Employee Benefits Security Administration Regional Director Ke
 lli Hammerl in Chicago. “The Department of Labor helped participants gai
 n access to their retirement savings.” The order permanently bars Turne
 r\, McCullough\, Nelson\, and Joslyn from serving as fiduciaries or servic
 e providers for any ERISA-covered employee benefit plan. The plan transfer
 red account balances belonging to plan participants it could not locate to
  the Pension Benefit Guaranty Corporation’s Missing Participants Progra
 m.Employers can get help complying with the law through EBSA’s correcti
 on programs\, which includes the Voluntary Fiduciary Correction Program. T
 he VFCP allows plan officials to correct violations and avoid excise taxes
  \nhttp://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/ebsa/ebsa20261026
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/ebsa/ebsa20261026
CATEGORIES:Federal Practice Areas
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ec4f4874878f26c4@fed-dol-news.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261026T223919Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261026
SUMMARY:[Federal Practice Areas] US Department of Labor awards $250\,000 i
 n Brookwood-Sago Grants to promote mine safety\, health education
DESCRIPTION:WASHINGTON – The U.S. Department of Labor today announced th
 e award of $250\,000 in Brookwood-Sago grant funding to two organizations 
 to support education and training initiatives that will help identify and 
 prevent unsafe working conditions in and around the nation’s mines.Admin
 istered by the department’s Mine Safety and Health Administration\, the
  Brookwood-Sago Mine Safety grant program helps recipients create trainin
 g materials\, conduct mine safety training\, and educational programs desi
 gned to protect the safety and health of miners. In awarding the grants\,
  MSHA focused on innovative plans for mine safety and pilot programs that 
 may be useful for miners working in a broad range of positions. The grants
  also put an emphasis on programs and materials targeting workers in small
 er mines that train mine operators and miners about MSHA standards\, and e
 mergency response training.Established under the Mine Improvement and New
  Emergency Response Act of 2006\, the grant program honors the miners los
 t at the Jim Walter Resources #5 mine in Brookwood\, Alabama\, on Sept. 23
 \, 2001\, and at the Sago Mine in Buckhannon\, West Virginia\, on Jan. 2\,
  2006.The recipients of the 2026 Brookwood-Sago grants are as follows:Reci
 pientCityState/TerritoryAmountSaelua Medical Corporation Pago PagoAmerica
 n Samoa$170\,000University of ArizonaTucsonAZ$80\,000\nhttp://www.dol.gov/
 newsroom/releases/msha/msha20261026
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/msha/msha20261026
CATEGORIES:Federal Practice Areas
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:fa5693fb6ea83453@fed-dol-news.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261024T043232Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261023
SUMMARY:[Federal Practice Areas] US Department of Labor\, US Trade Represe
 ntative secure another USMCA enforcement victory in Amphenol case
DESCRIPTION:WASHINGTON – The United States today announced the successf
 ul resolution of the USMCA Rapid Response Labor Mechanism (RRM) matter at 
 the Amphenol Optimize México\, S.A. de C.V. facility located in Nogales\
 , Sonora\, Mexico\, which manufactures automotive and aerospace connector
 s\, cables\, and components. The resolution is another win for the Trump 
 Administration\, whose America First approach ensures our trade partners d
 o not undermine worker protections to gain an unfair trade advantage or at
 tract investment.The United States and Mexico agreed on a course of remedi
 ation to address labor rights violations occurring at the facility\, inclu
 ding employer interference in union activity and retaliatory dismissals of
  workers. That plan has now been implemented\, and the Denial of Rights co
 ncerns raised in the U.S. request for review have been remediated. As a re
 sult\, the United States Trade Representative has directed the Secretary o
 f the Treasury to resume liquidation of unliquidated entries of goods from
  the facility.The U.S. Department of Labor’s Bureau of International Lab
 or Affairs\, the Office of the U.S. Trade Representative\, and the Mexican
  government facilitated a resolution with Amphenol to remediate workers’
  claims.Actions taken by the facility to address the matter include: Rein
 stating nine workers with full backpay and benefits\; Implementing a neutr
 ality statement and guidelines related to the freedom of association and c
 ollective bargaining\; andInstalling and publicizing a complaint mechanism
  through which workers can report violations of their rights and breaches 
 of company policies.Actions taken by the Government of Mexico to address t
 he matter include: Delivering in-person trainings for all company personn
 el on the freedom of association and collective bargaining\; Monitoring th
 e facility and engaging with workers and the company throughout its review
  period\; andMaintaining a direct email address\, reporting platform\, and
  telephone line for workers to anonymously re\nhttp://www.dol.gov/newsroom
 /releases/ilab/ilab20261023
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/ilab/ilab20261023
CATEGORIES:Federal Practice Areas
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:f0b657f3c578e3fe@fed-dol-news.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261022T213350Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261022
SUMMARY:[Federal Practice Areas] US Department of Labor secures court orde
 r requiring New Jersey employer to pay $700K in back wages\, damages to 48
  workers denied overtime pay
DESCRIPTION:VOORHEES\, NJ – The U.S. Department of Labor has obtained 
 a consent judgment and final order in federal district court requiring a N
 ew Jersey drywall installer to pay $725\,000 in back wages\, damages\, and
  civil money penalties after denying 48 workers overtime pay.The departmen
 t’s Wage and Hour Division found that P & B Partitions Inc. failed to pa
 y their employees for pre-shift work while they waited for transportation 
 to their jobsites. The failure to pay constitutes a violation under the Fa
 ir Labor Standards Act’s overtime provisions.  “Tasks completed to p
 repare for the job\, and in this case\, while waiting to get to the worksi
 te\, is work that must be compensated\,” said Wage and Hour Division Dis
 trict Director Charlene Rachor in Southern New Jersey. “Failing to pay w
 orkers overtime wages deprives them of their rights\, and we will hold emp
 loyers accountable for such actions.”The consent judgment requires P & B
  Partitions Inc. to pay $350\,000 in back wages\, an equal amount in liqui
 dated damages to 48 workers\, and requires payment of an additional $25\,0
 00 in civil penalties. The judgment was entered in the U.S. District Court
  for the District of New Jersey on June 26\, 2026.“The U.S. Department o
 f Labor will pursue legal action to ensure that all employees receive the 
 compensation they are legally owed\,” said Regional Solicitor of Labor J
 effrey S. Rogoff in New York. “This case demonstrates that employers can
  face costly consequences if they do not comply with federal wage law.”E
 mployees and employers can contact the Wage and Hour Division with questio
 ns and requests for compliance assistance at its toll-free helpline\, 86
 6-4US-WAGE (487-9243). Employers are encouraged to use the agency’s indu
 stry-specific compliance assistance toolkits to learn about their respon
 sibilities under the laws enforced by the division. The agency’s PAID p
 rogram offers employers an opportunity to self-report and resolve potenti
 al minimum wage and overtime violations under the FLSA\, as well as\nhttp:
 //www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/whd/whd20261022
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/whd/whd20261022
CATEGORIES:Federal Practice Areas
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:2082e537ac2f234f@fed-dol-news.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261022T213350Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261022
SUMMARY:[Federal Practice Areas] Unemployment Insurance Weekly Claims Repo
 rt
DESCRIPTION:In the week ending October 17\, the advance figure for seasona
 lly adjusted initial claims was 194\,000\, a decrease of 6\,000 from the p
 revious week's revised level. The previous week's level was revised up by 
 1\,000 from 199\,000 to 200\,000. The 4-week moving average was 197\,750\,
  a decrease of 1\,000 from the previous week's revised average. The previo
 us week's average was revised up by 250 from 198\,500 to 198\,750.\nhttp:/
 /www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/eta/eta20261022
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/eta/eta20261022
CATEGORIES:Federal Practice Areas
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:e023974e1bf1ecdf@fed-dol-news.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261021T214007Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261021
SUMMARY:[Federal Practice Areas] US Department of Labor cites Illinois uti
 lity construction company for repeated exposure to trenching hazards
DESCRIPTION:CHICAGO – The U.S. Department of Labor has cited a Frankfort
 -based utility construction company for repeatedly exposing employees to e
 xcavation safety hazards after federal investigators observed employees wo
 rking in an unprotected trench.The department’s Occupational Safety and 
 Health Administration initiated an investigation of Always Underground LLC
  as part of its National Emphasis Program on Trenching after witnessing em
 ployees working in an unprotected trench deeper than eight feet.OSHA cited
  the employer for three repeat violations for failing to provide workers w
 ith a safe means of egress\, to install a trench protective system that wo
 uld protect against cave-ins\, and to keep excavated or other materials an
 d equipment at least 2 feet from the edge. Additionally\, the agency cited
  the employer for a serious violation for failing to provide workers with 
 protective hardhats against falling objects.In total\, OSHA proposed $380\
 ,664 in penalties.The employer has 15 business days from receipt of its ci
 tations and penalties to comply\, request an informal conference with OSHA
 ’s area director\, or contest the findings before the independent Occupa
 tional Safety and Health Review Commission. Please check the OSHA establis
 hment search page periodically for any changes in the inspection or penalt
 y status.Visit OSHA’s website to learn about keeping workers safe around
  trenching and excavations. Employers can also contact the agency for info
 rmation about OSHA’s compliance assistance resources and for free help o
 n complying with OSHA standards.\nhttp://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/osh
 a/osha20261021
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/osha/osha20261021
CATEGORIES:Federal Practice Areas
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:f632b92de1511755@fed-dol-news.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261021T214007Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261021
SUMMARY:[Federal Practice Areas] US departments of Labor\, Health and Huma
 n Services expand efforts to help employees access health benefits
DESCRIPTION:WASHINGTON – The departments of Labor and Health and Human 
 Services announced that representatives from several large employers with 
 self-insured health plans signed a statement of support for the expansion 
 of electronic prior authorization reform to the self-insured markets. “
 The departments appreciate these U.S. companies for stepping forward to co
 llaborate with Labor and Health and Human Services to help employees cut t
 hrough red tape and receive their health benefits\,” said Assistant Secr
 etary for Employee Benefits Security Daniel Aronowitz. “By signing this 
 statement of support for the expansion of electronic prior authorization r
 eform\, government and private industry are working together on behalf of 
 the American workforce.”Signed by five delegates from Amazon\, Axon Ente
 rprise Inc.\, Google\, Meta\, and Oracle Corp.\, the statement supports th
 e June 2025 Prior Authorization Pledge\, which contains six key reforms\,
  including standardizing all prior authorization submissions\, reducing th
 e volume of medical procedures that require preapproval\, and making sure 
 medical professionals review all clinical denials. It also asks third part
 y administrators to enhance transparency and communication around authoriz
 ation decisions and appeals.“Patients and doctors shouldn’t have to fa
 ll back on faxes and paperwork simply because of how a patient’s health 
 plan is funded\,” said CMS Administrator Dr. Mehmet Oz. “We’ve broug
 ht insurers\, providers\, and technology companies to the table to moderni
 ze prior authorization\, and now employers are helping us take another imp
 ortant step. When the entire healthcare system moves together\, we can spe
 nd less time on paperwork and more time getting patients the care they nee
 d.”Over the past year\, the Trump Administration and industry partners h
 ave implemented electronic prior authorization reforms. In 2025\, leading 
 insurance companies signed the Prior Authorization Pledge to improve the p
 rocess for their Medicare Advantage\, Medicaid Managed Care\,\nhttp://www.
 dol.gov/newsroom/releases/ebsa/ebsa20261021
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/ebsa/ebsa20261021
CATEGORIES:Federal Practice Areas
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:eecd49abb937f346@fed-dol-news.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261019T222103Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261019
SUMMARY:[Federal Practice Areas] US Department of Labor awards more than $
 10.5M in grants to support mine safety\, health training across the nation
DESCRIPTION:WASHINGTON – The U.S. Department of Labor announced today t
 hat its Mine Safety and Health Administration has awarded more than $10.5 
 million in grants to reduce mining accidents\, injuries\, and illnesses by
  supporting training and education programs.The agency is awarding these g
 rants to support the delivery of federally mandated training for new and e
 xperienced miners at surface and underground coal and metal and nonmetal m
 ines. MSHA is awarding grants in 45 states\, the Navajo Nation\, and the C
 ommonwealth of the Northern Mariana Islands.“Thanks to President Trump
 ’s leadership we’re investing billions of dollars into critical mining
  projects and resources for mining education\,” said Secretary of Labor 
 Keith Sonderling. “Today’s grant announcement furthers the Department
 ’s goal to expand mining across the United States to support this Admini
 stration’s goals to provide critical resources to the workers industries
  that continue to expand in America.”States and territories apply for gr
 ant funding\, which is then awarded to state mine inspectors’ offices\, 
 state departments of labor\, and state-supported colleges and universities
 . Training and education programs are tailored locally to address conditio
 ns and hazards miners may experience.“A strong and thriving America reli
 es on a mining industry that safely produces the minerals essential to our
  economy\,” said Assistant Secretary for Mine Safety and Health Wayne Pa
 lmer. “These grants will promote safe and healthful workplaces for over 
 300\,000 miners across 47 states\, territories\, and tribal lands.”Learn
  more about MSHA.The recipients of MSHA State Grants are as follows: Reci
 pientCityState/TerritoryAwardBevill State Community College JasperAL $24
 3\,711University of Alaska at FairbanksFairbanksAK $160\,886Arizona State
  Mine Inspector’s OfficePhoenixAZ $453\,633Navajo Nation Minerals Depar
 tmentWindow RockAZ $46\,430Arkansas Department of Labor and LicensingLitt
 le Rock AR $138\,903Central Connecticut State UniversityNew BritainCT $
 98\nhttp://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/msha/msha20261019
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/msha/msha20261019
CATEGORIES:Federal Practice Areas
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:2f18aa85c00c3393@fed-dol-news.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261019T222103Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261019
SUMMARY:[Federal Practice Areas] Consent judgment orders California-based 
 home appliance company to pay $800K to 339 employees denied overtime pay
DESCRIPTION:LOS ANGELES – The U.S. Department of Labor has obtained a c
 onsent judgment requiring a home appliance company to pay $800\,000 in bac
 k wages and damages to 339 employees after an investigation found the comp
 any violated federal wage laws.The judgment comes after the department’s
  Wage and Hour Division investigated Coway USA Inc. and found that the emp
 loyer paid employees on a piece-rate basis and failed to pay employees for
  time spent attending mandatory meetings\, picking up and loading supplies
 \, and performing work before and after their shifts\, in violation of the
  Fair Labor Standards Act. Investigators also found that Coway did not inc
 lude nondiscretionary incentives\, bonuses\, and commissions in overtime p
 ay calculations and frequently reduced the number of hours employees recor
 ded working on their timecards.Entered in the U.S. District Court for the 
 Central District of California on Sept. 9\, the consent judgment requires 
 Coway to record all hours the implicated employees worked\, including time
  they spend picking up supplies\, attending mandatory training\, and commu
 nicating with customers before and after appointments. Coway is also requi
 red to give employees pay statements that show their hours worked\, servic
 es provided to customers\, and the rate Coway paid for the hours and servi
 ces. The consent judgment orders Coway USA Inc. to pay $400\,000 in unpai
 d overtime wages and an equal amount in damages to 339 employees working i
 n the greater metro areas of Los Angeles\, New York City\, and Washington\
 , D.C. The company also agreed to pay $10\,000 in penalties for willful vi
 olations. Coway USA Inc. is a subsidiary of Coway Co. Ltd.\, a multinatio
 nal company specializing in home appliances. The company installs and main
 tains various appliances\, including water and air filtration products thr
 oughout the U.S.Employers and employees can contact the Wage and Hour Divi
 sion with questions and requests for compliance assistance at its toll-fre
 e helpline\, 866-4US-WAGE (487-9243). Emp\nhttp://www.dol.gov/newsroom/rel
 eases/whd/whd20261019
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/whd/whd20261019
CATEGORIES:Federal Practice Areas
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:d72cd861bee1a5d8@fed-dol-news.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261019T222103Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261019
SUMMARY:[Federal Practice Areas] US Department of Labor awards $95.5M in g
 rants to support innovative programs for reskilling\, reemploying dislocat
 ed workers
DESCRIPTION:WASHINGTON – The U.S. Department of Labor today announced th
 e award of $95.5 million in funding to support the creation of innovativ
 e models of training and employment services that connect workers affected
  by layoffs to high-growth industries.Administered by the department’s 
 Employment and Training Administration\, Rapid Reskill Dislocated Worker G
 rants support 20 projects across 17 states to quickly connect workers affe
 cted by layoffs to employment and training services that lead to high-wage
 \, in-demand occupations in industries expected to grow\, including domest
 ic manufacturing\, aerospace\, energy production\, construction\, and othe
 r occupations important to reindustrialization.Reskill DWG projects are de
 signed to swiftly assess participants’ prior experience and existing ski
 llsets and chart a course to an in-demand occupation or industry. Particip
 ants will gain industry-specific skills and credentials to speed their ree
 mployment while projects will develop and share innovative approaches for 
 connecting workers impacted by layoffs with careers in America’s growth 
 industries.“The Rapid Reskill Grants support our commitment to ensuring 
 displaced workers have the skills they need to pursue the American Dream\,
 ” said Secretary of Labor Keith Sonderling. “They also help ensure a w
 orkforce for the Golden Age of reindustrialization and empower workers to 
 stay rooted in their hometown.”Reskill DWGs aim to rapidly place disloca
 ted workers into paid work\, whether full-time unsubsidized employment\, a
  Registered Apprenticeship\, or other paid\, structured on-the-job trainin
 g in their target occupation or industry within six months of enrollment.
  Toward that end\, these innovative projects incorporate short-term\, inte
 nsive training programs that lead to industry-recognized credentials and s
 kills development aligned with industry-specific or occupational training.
  Supported by the Workforce Innovation and Opportunity Act\, National Di
 slocated Worker Grants provide funding assistance t\nhttp://www.dol.gov/n
 ewsroom/releases/eta/eta20261019
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/eta/eta20261019
CATEGORIES:Federal Practice Areas
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:72a48630a55cffee@fed-dol-news.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261019T222103Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261019
SUMMARY:[Federal Practice Areas] US Department of Labor announces field as
 sistance bulletin withdrawing 3 opinion letters on tipped employees
DESCRIPTION:WASHINGTON – The U.S. Department of Labor’s Wage and Hour
  Division today announced the issuance of a new field assistance bulletin 
 addressing the treatment of related duties performed by tipped employees u
 nder the Fair Labor Standards Act. Field Assistance Bulletin No. 2026-1 
 brings the division’s guidance on tipped employees with dual jobs into a
 lignment with current regulations.The bulletin withdraws the division’s 
 1979\, 1980\, and 1985 opinion letters concerning the application of dual 
 jobs regulations to tipped employees. The withdrawal of the three opinion 
 letters is necessary because they no longer reflect the division’s polic
 y or interpretation of the FLSA. The bulletin also clarifies that the curr
 ent dual jobs regulation focuses on whether an employee performs duties re
 lated to the employee’s tipped occupation\, rather than imposing a speci
 fic percentage-based limit on the time an employee may spend performing re
 lated\, non-tipped duties within that occupation.The publication of the ne
 w bulletin follows a 2024 federal court decision that vacated the departme
 nt’s 2021 dual jobs final rule. After that decision\, the department rem
 oved the regulatory text adopted under the 2021 rule and reinstated the or
 iginal dual jobs regulations.The new field assistance bulletin clarifies t
 hat no current Wage and Hour Division guidance relies on the former percen
 tage-based limit on the time a tipped employee may spend performing relate
 d\, non-tipped duties\, commonly known as the 80/20 rule. The division rem
 oved its 80/20 rule guidance from its Field Operations Handbook in Januar
 y 2025\, and the handbook is now consistent with the reinstated 1967 dual 
 jobs regulations.As noted in the most recent regulatory agenda\, the depa
 rtment is developing a notice of proposed rulemaking to amend various regu
 lations for tipped employees under the FLSA. The department welcomes publi
 c comments on the proposed rule once it is published.Learn more about the 
 Wage and Hour Division\, including the agen\nhttp://www.dol.gov/newsroom/
 releases/whd/whd20261019-0
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/whd/whd20261019-0
CATEGORIES:Federal Practice Areas
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:0d6172f74edfa978@fed-dol-news.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261016T210457Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261016
SUMMARY:[Federal Practice Areas] US Department of Labor awards $81.5M in g
 rants to support industry-driven job training programs in rural communitie
 s
DESCRIPTION:WASHINGTON – The U.S. Department of Labor today announced th
 e award of $81.5 million in funding to address the critical workforce nee
 ds of employers in high-growth and emerging industries across the Appalach
 ian\, Delta\, and Northern Border regions. Administered by the departmen
 t’s Employment and Training Administration\, the seventh round of Work
 force Opportunity for Rural Communities program funding supports 13 projec
 ts across 11 states to stimulate employment and wage growth\, as well as e
 conomic mobility\, self-sufficiency\, and increased labor force participat
 ion. The demonstration grants support the workforce needs of regional empl
 oyers in predominantly rural areas to enable them to better compete econom
 ically and enable residents of the regions to obtain or retain family-sust
 aining employment opportunities so they can remain and thrive in their com
 munities.“These grants ensure those in our rural communities have a path
 way to the training they need for in-demand jobs that feed a family and st
 rengthen our supply chains\,” said Acting Secretary of Labor Keith Sonde
 rling. “They help us advance President Trump’s goal of building the wo
 rkforce for the Golden Age and enable more people to pursue the American D
 ream.”The WORC Initiative is made available in partnership with the App
 alachian Regional Commission\, the Delta Regional Authority\, and the N
 orthern Border Regional Commission.The grants will help provide industry-d
 riven training\, including Registered Apprenticeships\, for new and dislo
 cated workers\, and those already in the workforce who need to learn new 
 skills. These programs will focus on high-growth and emerging industry se
 ctors such as skilled trades and construction\, healthcare\, information t
 echnology\, manufacturing\, domestic energy production\, and business and 
 professional services. Grants are being awarded to recipients as follows:
 RecipientStateAmountTuskegee UniversityAL$7\,500\,000Arkansas Rural Health
  Partnership Inc.AR$6\,133\,431Eastern Kentucky Concentrated E\nhttp://www
 .dol.gov/newsroom/releases/eta/eta20261016
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/eta/eta20261016
CATEGORIES:Federal Practice Areas
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
